<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Empty, and that is the statement: no iCloud, no keychain sharing, no app
      groups. The vault stays in this app's container, sealed under a key
      inside the Secure Enclave, and there is nowhere for it to sync to.

      An entitlement named here has to be authorised by the provisioning
      profile that signs the build, even when it grants nothing. Declaring an
      empty iCloud array therefore asked for a capability the app does not use
      and that a personal team cannot sign, which would have failed the first
      install on a device. The absence of a key is what refusing a capability
      looks like.
    -->

    <!--
      The app's own keychain group, and nothing else's. This is not a
      capability that shares anything: the value expands to this app's
      identifier, which is the group it would get anyway from the profile that
      signs a build for a device.

      It is here because without it a build with no team has no group at all,
      and every keychain write fails with a missing entitlement. That is what
      refused to create a wallet on the simulator: not the Enclave, not the
      passcode, an app that had never been signed.
    -->
    <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)$(CFBundleIdentifier)</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
